$DIR/main.p:19:12: v is moved to the task started here, but a view of what it holds (v.Slice(), line 18) was stored in keep at line 18, where the compiler cannot check its uses
    An owner may move to another task only when no other alias of the memory it
    holds is left behind (language spec section 8.8). The view of v made by
    v.Slice() at line 18 went into memory the compiler does not follow, so this
    task could still use it while the task that receives v does.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Keep the view in a local variable or a field of one, where the compiler
    follows it, or do not hand it to a call that keeps it
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3314" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:18:2: cannot store v.Slice() in package variable keep, which outlives v
    v.Slice() points into v. v is a local variable of main (declared at
    main.p:16). When main returns, v's memory is reclaimed, but package
    variables live for the whole program, so package variable keep would be left
    pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store values instead of views of v, or allocate v and store its owner, an
    owned[pluto/vec.Vec[int]], which moves there:
        keep = new(pluto/vec.Vec[int])
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
